{#
  ABOUT ME — Figma 185:2029. Content from _data/about.js.

  TWO COLUMNS of equal width inside the same 120px gutters as the header and
  hero, so the page frame runs unbroken down the page. The rules are part of
  that frame: the outer pair continue the header's, and the comp adds two more
  inside — a divider between the columns and one above the time widget.

  THE TIME WIDGET IS STATIC. Every value is mocked from the comp. The
  data-clock-* hooks are the contract for the later phase that makes it live:
  scripts/clock.js already computes exactly these three fields, so that phase
  is a rewire, not a rewrite. Do not rename them casually.
#}

{% from "components/stat.njk" import stat %}

<section class="about" id="about-me" data-header-theme="light">
  <div class="about__inner">
    <div class="about__figure-col">
      {#
        THREE LAYERS, frame behind the figure — the same order Figma draws them.
        The frame is an exported open path (see about.js); the figure is a
        transparent PNG that overhangs it at the bottom, so the arms read as
        crossing the line rather than sitting inside a box. The eyes sit on top;
        see the note on the overlay itself.
      #}
      <div class="about__figure">
        <img
          class="about__frame"
          src="{{ about.illustration.frame }}"
          alt=""
          aria-hidden="true"
          draggable="false"
        />
        <img
          class="about__illustration"
          src="{{ about.illustration.file }}"
          alt="{{ about.illustration.alt }}"
          draggable="false"
        />

        {#
          THE EYES, REDRAWN SO THEY CAN MOVE.

          The illustration is a raster — illustration-pari.svg is a wrapper
          around the very same PNG — and inside it the pupil is one dark blob
          FUSED to the lash line above it: no gap, no eye-white, no lower lid.
          Nothing in the file can be transformed, so each eye is rebuilt here in
          two parts: a face-coloured patch that takes the baked pupil out, and a
          fresh pupil that scripts/about-eyes.js translates.

          THE PATCH AND THE PUPIL ARE THE SAME ELLIPSE, one painted in the
          face's white and one in the drawing's ink, and only the second moves.
          At rest they sit on top of each other and neither is visible; slide
          the pupil and the patch behind it becomes the white of an eye looking
          sideways. See about.js for why they must be identical rather than the
          patch being the safer, bigger one.

          THE PATCH IS CLIPPED TO BELOW THE LASH, per-eye, because the lash's
          underside is level over the pupil and then dives at the outer corner.
          The clip is what keeps the patch off the lash; it is not what sizes
          it. Both polygons are measured — see about.js.

          The pupil is deliberately NOT clipped: its top half sits over the lash
          and merges into it, which is how the original reads and what keeps it
          from looking like a dot resting below an eye.

          #FDFDFD IS THE ARTWORK'S OWN WHITE, sampled off the rendered page —
          the face is 253,253,253 at full alpha, not #fff, and a pure white
          patch shows up as a bright disc against it.

          The coordinates are the 528x622 space the figure already uses, and
          they are post-mirror: .about__illustration is flipped with scale:-1 1,
          so the viewer's left eye is the one with the larger x.
        #}
        <svg
          class="about__eyes"
          viewBox="0 0 528 622"
          aria-hidden="true"
          focusable="false"
          data-about-eyes
        >
          <defs>
            {%- for eye in about.illustration.eyes %}
              <clipPath id="about-eye-clip-{{ loop.index }}">
                <polygon points="{{ eye.clip }}" />
              </clipPath>
            {%- endfor %}
          </defs>
          {%- for eye in about.illustration.eyes %}
            <ellipse
              class="about__eye-patch"
              clip-path="url(#about-eye-clip-{{ loop.index }})"
              cx="{{ eye.cx }}"
              cy="{{ eye.cy }}"
              rx="{{ eye.rx }}"
              ry="{{ eye.ry }}"
            />
          {%- endfor %}
          {%- for eye in about.illustration.eyes %}
            <ellipse
              class="about__pupil"
              data-pupil
              cx="{{ eye.cx }}"
              cy="{{ eye.cy }}"
              rx="{{ eye.rx }}"
              ry="{{ eye.ry }}"
            />
          {%- endfor %}
        </svg>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="about__body">
      <div class="about__content">
        <h2 class="about__heading">
          <span class="about__heading-lead">{{ about.heading.lead }}</span>
          <span class="about__heading-rest">{{ about.heading.rest }}</span>
        </h2>

        <ul class="about__stats">
          {%- for s in about.stats %}{{ stat(s.value, s.label) }}{% endfor %}
        </ul>

        {%- for para in about.bio %}
          <p class="about__bio">{{ para }}</p>
        {%- endfor %}

        <p class="about__location">
          <img
            class="about__flag"
            src="{{ site.flag }}"
            alt=""
            aria-hidden="true"
            width="16"
            height="16"
          />
          {{ about.location }}
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="about__clock" data-clock>
        <p class="about__clock-time">
          <span class="about__clock-at">{{ about.clock.atLabel }}</span>
          <span class="about__clock-value" data-clock-time>{{ about.clock.time }}</span>
        </p>
        <p class="about__clock-status">
          {#
            NOT IN THE COMP. Figma 185:2029 draws no indicator — the v2 sidebar
            widget had one at the tip of an L-connector and the brief asks for
            it back, so it is added here rather than invented silently. It is
            one span and one CSS block; delete both to return to the comp.
          #}
          <span class="about__clock-dot" data-clock-dot aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <span class="about__clock-lead" data-clock-lead>{{ about.clock.statusLead }}</span>
          <span class="about__clock-headline" data-clock-status>{{ about.clock.status }}</span>
        </p>
        <p class="about__clock-detail" data-clock-detail>{{ about.clock.detail }}</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
